    "https://jobs.ferris.edu/en-us/job/498248/david-l-eisler-ctr-student-manager-student-position": {
      "title": "David L. Eisler Ctr Student Manager (Student Position)",
      "description": "David L. Eisler Ctr Student Manager (Student Position) Apply now Job no: 498248 Work type: Student Employment - General Location: Big Rapids, MI 49307 Categories: Student Employment - General Position Title: University Center Student Manager (Student Position) Location: Big Rapids, MI 49307 Department: Student Services Advertised Salary: $14.00- $14.49 Summary of Position: Under the supervision of Associate Dean of Student Life, the Student Manager is responsible for the general operation of the David L. Eisler Center during the evenings and on weekends. Evening shifts are generally from 5-10:15pm and weekends shifts are generally from 9:45am-3:00pm and 3:00pm-9/10:00pm Position Requirements: - Must be able to work weekday nights (5-10:15pm) and weekend shifts. - Must be able to lift 50lbs to shoulder level. - Must be able to do other physical work including standing, lifting, stacking and moving equipment. Essential Duties/Responsibilities: - Carrying out David L Eisler Center policies - Providing customer service in a professional matter and hold a positive image. - Assist guests with making reservations. - Ensure the David L. Eisler Center is locked and unlocked as appropriate for opening and closing the facility. - Ensure that events are setup to guests expectations. - Enforce and uphold David L. Eisler Center policies. - Be able to move and set up audiovisual equipment. - Work with fulltime and student staff to ensure events are properly set. - Able to do manual set ups of event spaces. - Responsible for vacuuming, window cleaning and other cleaning as needed. - Other duties as assigned by the Night Manager or Director of the University Center. Number of Positions Available: 2 Documents Needed to Apply: Resume/ Class Schedule Job Close Date: October 16, 2026 EEO Statement: Ferris State University, an Equal Opportunity employer, is committed to cultivating an environment of belonging that values, respects, supports, and celebrates individual similarities and differences within its community, allowing students, faculty, and staff to thrive authentically. Ferris offers employment opportunities to qualified candidates seeking careers in a student-focused environment that values opportunity, collaboration, diversity and educational excellence. Learn more about the Ferris Mission and community at ferris.edu . For more information on the University’s Policy on Non-Discrimination, visit: Ferris Non-Discrimination Statement . Advertised: 21 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 16 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://jobs.ferris.edu/en-us/job/498233/director-of-transfer-and-articulation": {
      "title": "Director of Transfer and Articulation",
      "description": "Director of Transfer and Articulation Apply now Job no: 498233 Work type: Staff Location: Big Rapids, MI 49307 Categories: Administrative, Staff Position Title: Director of Transfer and Articulation Location: Big Rapids (Main Campus) Department: 40701 - CES Student Success Advertised Salary: $89,471. See Job Summary for details on salary placement. Salary commensurate with education, experience, and other requirements. Benefits: Comprehensive benefit package (health care, vacation, etc.). Please see the following link for a list of benefits offered with this position. Admin / Admin Temp Benefit Plans FLSA: Exempt Temporary/Continuing: Continuing Part-Time/Full-Time: Full-Time Union Group: N/A Term of Position: 12 Month At Will/Just Cause: At Will Summary of Position: The Director of Transfer and Articulation provides strategic leadership and day-to-day operational oversight for the University’s transfer credit, articulation agreements, and credit-for-prior-learning functions. Reporting to the Associate Dean of the College of Extended Studies, the Director is accountable for ensuring that every credit a student has already earned – at a community college, four-year institution, through an industry credential or apprenticeship, or through documented professional experience – is evaluated accurately, awarded consistently, and posted in time to be useful to the student. This role requires operational precision and relationship-building in equal measure. The Director owns the institutional processes behind articulated credit, credit for credential, and the Assessment of Prior Learning portfolio, while simultaneously serving as Ferris State’s primary articulation contact for Michigan’s community colleges and peer institutions. The Director represents the University in statewide transfer work, including the Michigan Transfer Agreement (MTA), the Michigan Transfer Network, MiTransfer and MiWorkforce Pathways, and MACRAO. The anticipated start date for this position is November of 2026. However, this is an estimated date and can be revised if needed. The selected candidate must be authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire without employer assistance or sponsorship now or in the future. The university will not assist with any F-1 EADs, OPT, H1B, etc. Offers are determined based on a review of the candidate's qualifications, including education, training, experience, and internal pay equity considerations. Placement within the range will generally be based on the following factors: Entry-Range: Candidates who meet the minimum qualifications and core requirements of the position. Mid-Range: Candidates with three or more years of directly related professional experience and demonstrate proficiency in the key responsibilities of the role. Upper Range: Candidates with seven or more years of directly related experience, advanced knowledge, specialized skills, certifications, or expertise that provide significant value to the position. Final compensation will be determined based on the candidate's overall qualifications, experience, and alignment with the University's compensation philosophy in consultation with Human Resources. Position Type: Staff Required Education: Master’s degree in higher education administration, education, student affairs, counseling, business, or a related field. Required Work Experience: Five years of progressively responsible professional experience in higher education in transfer credit evaluation, articulation, admissions, records and registration, academic advising, prior learning assessment, or a closely related area. Demonstrated experience developing, negotiating, or administering articulation agreements, transfer guides, or course equivalency processes. Demonstrated experience leading projects, teams, or cross-departmental processes. Experience working in a student information system, degree audit, transfer articulation, or a functionally related system. Required Licenses and Certifications: Physical Demands: Office Environment Carrying Moving Sitting Lifting Additional Education/Experiences to be Considered: Experience with Michigan’s statewide transfer framework, including the Michigan Transfer Agreement, the Michigan Transfer Network, MiTransfer and MiWorkforce Pathways, and MACRAO. Knowledge of CAEL standards for prior learning assessment and experience administering portfolio-based credit. Experience awarding credit for industry credentials, AP, CLEP, IB. Working knowledge of Banner, Ellucian, or comparable enterprise student systems, and of transfer evaluation tools such as TES or Transferology. Essential Duties/Responsibilities: Strategy, and Supervision - Provide vision, leadership, and direction for all transfer credit, articulation, and credit for prior learning functions across Ferris State University. - Develop, document, and continuously improve written policies, procedures, and process maps for every credit-award pathway within the portfolio. - Manage the operating budget; recommend staffing, technology, and resource investments that improve turnaround time and accuracy. - Partner with the Registrar, Admissions, Financial Aid, Academic Advising, deans, department chairs, and faculty to resolve credit questions and align practice with academic policy. - Establish and publish annual transfer and articulation metrics including transfer credit volume, evaluation turnaround time, agreement status, and award rates and report the results on a defined cycle. - Serve on University committees related to transfer, curriculum, enrollment, and student success, and lead the campus articulation review process with academic departments. - Train, evaluate, and develop professional and support staff and student employees; establish clear workload distribution, coverage plans, and cross-training so that no critical process depends on a single individual. Articulation Agreements and Transfer Guides - Lead the development, negotiation, execution, maintenance, and renewal of articulation agreements with Michigan community colleges, in-state and out-of-state institutions, in coordination with academic colleges and the appropriate University signatories. - Serve as Ferris State’s institutional contact for the Michigan Transfer Agreement (MTA), the Michigan Transfer Network, MiTransfer and MiWorkforce Pathways, and MACRAO, including keeping the University’s institutional profile and course equivalency data current. - Manage the course equivalency database and the departmental review workflow, ensuring new and revised equivalency decisions are returned by faculty within established timeframes and recorded in the student information system. - Develop and maintain student-facing transfer guides and equivalency resources that are accurate, accessible, and updated on a published schedule. - Support reverse transfer, guest student, and degree-completion pathways in partnership with community college colleagues. - Monitor the articulation agreement portfolio for expiration and renewal dates, initiating renewals proactively and maintaining organized records of executed agreements. Transfer Credit Evaluation - Oversee the evaluation of all post-secondary transfer credit, ensuring decisions are made accurately, applied consistently with academic policy, and completed within published turnaround standards. - Manage the course equivalency review workflow with academic departments, ensuring timely faculty decisions on new courses and maintaining a complete, current record of all established equivalencies. - Ensure transfer credit is recorded correctly in the student information system and is visible to students and advisors in time to be used for registration and degree planning. - Establish and publish clear service standards for transfer credit evaluation, including turnaround times, student communication protocols, and appeal procedures. Credit for Prior Learning - Provide overall leadership for the University’s credit for prior learning (CPL) portfolio, ensuring practice remains consistent with the standards of the Council for Adult and Experiential Learning (CAEL), Higher Learning Commission expectations, and Ferris State academic policy. - Oversee the Assessment of Prior Learning (APL) portfolio process, including student intake, advising on options, portfolio development support, coordination of faculty assessment, and posting of resulting credit. - Maintain and publish clear, student-facing information on every CPL pathway, including eligibility, cost, timeline, and the limits of transferability to other institutions. - Ensure prior learning credit is recorded accurately in the student information system and reflected correctly in degree audit. Credit for Credential - Administer the Credit for Credential program, evaluating industry certifications, licenses, apprenticeships, registered training programs, and other third-party credentials for college credit. - Work with academic departments and industry partners to identify high-value credentials, determine appropriate credit awards, and document approved credential-to-credit equivalencies in a maintained, publicly available crosswalk. - Establish verification standards for credential authenticity and currency, including handling of expired or lapsed credentials. - Review the credential crosswalk on a defined cycle as industry standards, certifying bodies, and Ferris curriculum change. - Support workforce, adult learner, and degree-completion enrollment strategies by making credential-based credit visible early in the recruitment and admission process. - Carries out responsibilities in accordance with university policies and applicable laws. - Cultivate an environment of belonging that values, respects, supports, and celebrates individual similarities and differences, allowing students, faculty, and staff to thrive authentically. - Support, promote, and develop university student enrollment and retention initiatives. - Any other duties assigned within the position classification area. Marginal Duties/Responsibilities: Skills and Abilities: Exceptional attention to detail and the ability to apply complex policy accurately and consistently across a high volume of individual student cases. Ability to work simultaneously across multiple cyclical deadlines and to protect fixed operational deadlines such as registration, orientation and term start. Strong written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to explain technical credit policy clearly to students, families, faculty, and external partners. Demonstrated ability to build and sustain productive relationships across institutional boundaries, including with faculty, community college colleagues, and state agencies. Sound independent judgment in evaluating coursework, credentials, and experiential learning claims where institutional policy requires interpretation. Commitment to equitable credit evaluation practices and to removing unnecessary barriers for students who arrive with prior learning from non-traditional pathways. Demonstrated successful experience working directly with people from diverse backgrounds, including cultural, educational, socioeconomic, and life experiences. Required Documents: Cover Letter Resume Unofficial Transcript 1 Optional Documents: Special Instructions to Applicants: Unofficial Transcript 1 (REQUIRED): Applicants must attach a copy of an unofficial transcript of a Master's degree. Transcripts must include the institution name, the applicant's name, the date the degree was attained, and the degree awarded. Finalists will be required to submit an official transcript. Initial Application Review Date: October 5, 2026 Open Until Position is Filled?: Yes Posting Close Date: EEO Statement: Ferris State University, an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity employer, is committed to enhancing equity, inclusion, and diversity within its community. Ferris offers employment opportunities to qualified candidates seeking careers in a student-focused environment that values opportunity, collaboration, diversity and educational excellence. Learn more about the Ferris Mission and community at ferris.edu . The University actively seeks applications from women, minorities, individuals with disabilities, veterans, and other underrepresented groups. For more information on the University’s Policy on Non-Discrimination, visit: Ferris Non-Discrimination Statement . Click here to learn more about working at FSU and KCAD. Advertised: 18 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://jobs.ferris.edu/en-us/job/498236/adjunct-clinical-parttime": {
      "title": "Adjunct - Clinical (Part-Time)",
      "description": "Adjunct - Clinical (Part-Time) Apply now Job no: 498236 Work type: Faculty - Temporary & Continuing Location: Big Rapids, MI 49307 Categories: Faculty Position Title: Adjunct - Clinical (Part-Time) Location: Big Rapids (Main Campus) Department: 36200 - Optometry Clinic Instruction Advertised Salary: Tier 1: $55.59; Tier 2: $63.83; Tier 3: $72.07 per hour, determined by: education, residency, fellowship, years of clinical experience, and scholarly activities. The hourly rate will be evaluated and determined by MCO in accordance with defined criteria. Benefits: Comprehensive benefit package (health care, vacation, etc.). Please see the following link for a list of benefits offered with this position. Adjunct Faculty FLSA: Exempt Temporary/Continuing: Temporary Part-Time/Full-Time: Part-Time Union Group: N/A Term of Position: 12 Month At Will/Just Cause: Just Cause Summary of Position: Provide in-person clinical teaching and patient care at the University Eye Center campus clinic (Big Rapids). The candidate(s) selected for the position must reside in Michigan after acceptance of employment. This is a pooled position. One or more applicants may be hired as needed to provide clinical education on a semester-by-semester basis. Compensation: Tier* Compensation Range/ Education/training/ Work Experience/ Scholarly Tier 1 ($55.59/hour) Optometry (O.D. Degree) without Residency; and 2 years experience Tier 2 ($63.83/hour) Optometry (O.D. Degree) with Residency ; and 3 years experience; OR Fellowship (FAAO, FOVDRA, etc.) or equivalent Tier 3 ($72.07/hour) Optometry (O.D. Degree) with Residency; and 5 years experience; and Fellowship/certification; and national professional organization lectures/posters and/or other published works The anticipated start date of this position is October 2026; however, this is an estimated date and can be revised as needed. It will remain ongoing as needed. Position Type: Faculty - Temporary & Continuing Required Education: At the time of appointment, the applicant must hold the Doctor of Optometry (O.D.) degree. Required Work Experience: At least one year of residency experience or have at least 2 years in patient care work experience. Required Licenses and Certifications: At the time of appointment, the applicant must have or be eligible for a Michigan optometry license with DPA and TPA certifications for appointment to the college's clinical staff. Physical Demands: Office Environment Moving Reaching Sitting Standing Additional Education/Experiences to be Considered: Previous experience supervising and training optometry interns. Essential Duties/Responsibilities: Perform duties in patient care and clinical instruction. Clinical faculty have professional responsibilities in patient care and clinical instruction, which may include being accessible to students outside regular clinical assignment hours and participating in traditional functions of academic significance, such as assessing students' clinical proficiency in accordance with university systems and guidelines. Clinical faculty are required to provide in-person instruction. Participate in related FSU and/or college-specific training and development activities. Carries out responsibilities in accordance with university policies and applicable laws. Cultivates an environment of belonging that values, respects, supports, and celebrates individual similarities and differences, allowing students, faculty, and staff to thrive authentically. Support, promote, and develop university student enrollment and retention initiatives. Any other duties assigned within the position classification area. Marginal Duties/Responsibilities: Skills and Abilities: Applicant must possess excellent clinical, interpersonal, and communication skills. Demonstrated successful experience working directly with people from diverse backgrounds, including cultural, educational, socioeconomic, and life experiences. Required Documents: Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Unofficial Transcript 1 Certification/License Optional Documents: Other Additional Document Special Instructions to Applicants: Unofficial Transcript 1 (REQUIRED): Applicants must attach a copy of unofficial transcript of Optometry (O.D. Degree). Transcript must include: Institution name, applicant name, date degree attained, degree awarded. Finalist will be required to submit an official transcript. License (REQUIRED): Please attach copy of current O.D. license or proof of eligibility as described in the Required Licenses and Certifications section. Other Additional Document (OPTIONAL): Residency certificate or fellowship or other certifications. Applicants are advised to clearly detail in their application materials all applicable: education, residency, fellowship, years of clinical experience, and scholarly activities. Initial Application Review Date: October 5, 2026 Open Until Position is Filled?: Yes Posting Close Date: EEO Statement: Ferris State University, an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity employer, is committed to enhancing equity, inclusion, and diversity within its community. Ferris offers employment opportunities to qualified candidates seeking careers in a student-focused environment that values opportunity, collaboration, diversity and educational excellence. Learn more about the Ferris Mission and community at ferris.edu . The University actively seeks applications from women, minorities, individuals with disabilities, veterans, and other underrepresented groups. For more information on the University’s Policy on Non-Discrimination, visit: Ferris Non-Discrimination Statement . Click here to learn more about working at FSU and KCAD. Advertised: 18 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://jobs.ferris.edu/en-us/job/498243/museum-gallery-stu-assistant-student-position": {
      "title": "Museum & Gallery Stu Assistant (Student Position)",
      "description": "Museum & Gallery Stu Assistant (Student Position) Apply now Job no: 498243 Work type: Student Employment - Work Study Only Location: Big Rapids, MI 49307 Categories: Student Employment - Work Study Only Position Title: Museums and Galleries Student Asst - Art Gallery (Student Position) Location: Big Rapids, MI Department: Art Gallery Advertised Salary: $13.73- $13.99 Summary of Position: Student workers will assist in all duties relating to the installation and striking of exhibitions using proper art handling methods and Best Practices. Position Requirements: - Applicants should possess basic computer and office skills - Effective interpersonal skills - good written and oral communication skills - Be able to lift and carry variety of items to set up the gallery Essential Duties/Responsibilities: - Interact positively and be able to discuss the art on display with a diverse demographic of visitors to the gallery including artists, students, staff, faculty, administration, and community members both youth and adult. - Assist in maintaining attendance records - Provide secretarial support and assist with updating and maintaining the gallery website and social media sites. Number of Positions Available: 1 Documents Needed to Apply: Resume/Class Schedule Job Close Date: December 12, 2026 EEO Statement: Ferris State University, an Equal Opportunity employer, is committed to cultivating an environment of belonging that values, respects, supports, and celebrates individual similarities and differences within its community, allowing students, faculty, and staff to thrive authentically. Ferris offers employment opportunities to qualified candidates seeking careers in a student-focused environment that values opportunity, collaboration, diversity and educational excellence. Learn more about the Ferris Mission and community at ferris.edu . For more information on the University’s Policy on Non-Discrimination, visit: Ferris Non-Discrimination Statement . Advertised: 18 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 12 Dec 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
