    "https://jobs.ferris.edu/en-us/job/498197/vice-president-governmental-external-affairs": {
      "title": "Vice President Governmental & External Affairs",
      "description": "Vice President Governmental & External Affairs Apply now Job no: 498197 Work type: Staff Location: Big Rapids, MI 49307 Categories: Administrative, Staff Position Title: Vice President Governmental & External Affairs Location: Big Rapids (Main Campus) Department: 21000 - Presidents Office Advertised Salary: $171,737, plus university issued vehicle. Benefits: Comprehensive benefit package (health care, vacation, etc.). Please see the following link for a list of benefits offered with this position. Admin / Admin Temp Benefit Plans FLSA: Exempt Temporary/Continuing: Continuing Part-Time/Full-Time: Full-Time Union Group: N/A Term of Position: 12 Month At Will/Just Cause: At Will Summary of Position: The Vice President for Governmental Relations & External Affairs provides strategic leadership for the University’s governmental relations, external affairs, and public policy efforts. Reporting to the President, this position serves as the University’s principal liaison with federal, state, and local government officials and agencies and works to advance the University’s mission, priorities, and interests through effective government and community engagement. The Vice President develops and implements comprehensive governmental relations strategies; monitors legislative and regulatory developments; builds and maintains relationships with elected and appointed officials; and represents the University before governmental entities and other external stakeholders. The position works closely with the President, Board of Trustees, senior leaders, faculty, staff, students, and external partners to identify opportunities, assess potential impacts of public policy, and coordinate the University’s response to legislative and regulatory issues. The Vice President also leads efforts to identify and pursue public funding opportunities, including state and federal appropriations, grants, capital projects, and other governmental resources that support the University’s strategic priorities. This position may have a primary work location in both Lansing and Big Rapids. The Vice President, in collaboration with the President, will build strategic relationships with businesses and corporations throughout Michigan, with a particular focus on Southeast and West Michigan. The selected candidate for this position must be authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire without employer assistance or sponsorship now or in the future. The university will not assist with any F-1 EADs, OPT, H1B, etc. Offers are determined based on a review of the candidate's qualifications, including education, training, experience, and internal pay equity considerations. Placement within the range will generally be based on the following factors: Entry-Range: Candidates who meet the minimum qualifications and core requirements of the position. Mid-Range: Candidates with three or more years of directly related professional experience and demonstrate proficiency in the key responsibilities of the role. Upper Range: Candidates with seven or more years of directly related experience, advanced knowledge, specialized skills, certifications, or expertise that provide significant value to the position. Final compensation will be determined based on the candidate's overall qualifications, experience, and alignment with the University's compensation philosophy in consultation with Human Resources. Position Type: Staff Required Education: A Bachelor’s Degree from an accredited institution. Required Work Experience: Significant progressively responsible experience in governmental relations, public policy, legislative affairs, higher education, government, public administration, or a related field. Demonstrated experience developing and implementing governmental or legislative strategies. Demonstrated experience building and maintaining effective relationships with elected officials, government agencies, legislative staff, and other external stakeholders. Excellent written, verbal, interpersonal, presentation, and negotiation skills. Demonstrated ability to analyze complex legislative and regulatory issues and communicate their implications to senior leadership. Demonstrated ability to work effectively in a highly collaborative, politically sensitive, and complex environment. Ability to represent the University effectively with a wide range of internal and external constituencies. Required Licenses and Certifications: Physical Demands: Office Environment Bending Carrying Sitting Twisting Balancing Driving Repetitive movement Standing Additional Education/Experiences to be Considered: Essential Duties/Responsibilities: *Develop and execute a comprehensive governmental relations strategy aligned with the University’s strategic plan, institutional priorities, and mission. *Serve as the University’s primary representative and advocate with federal, state, and local elected officials, government agencies, legislative staff, and other governmental stakeholders. *Establish and maintain strong, collaborative relationships with members of the state and federal legislature, local government leaders, and their staffs. *Monitor federal, state, and local legislation, regulations, policies, and governmental actions that may affect the University, higher education, students, employees, or the communities the University serves. *Advise the President, Board of Trustees, and senior leadership regarding legislative and public policy developments, emerging issues, risks, and opportunities. *Develop strategies and recommendations for advancing the University’s legislative and public policy priorities. *Coordinate the University’s response to proposed legislation, regulations, and governmental initiatives in collaboration with appropriate University leaders and subject matter experts. *Represent the University at legislative hearings, governmental meetings, policy forums, community events, and other external engagements. *Develop and maintain relationships with higher education associations, business and community organizations, advocacy groups, and other strategic partners to advance shared priorities. *Coordinate the development and delivery of legislative testimony, briefing materials, policy statements, talking points, correspondence, presentations, and other governmental relations communications. *Identify and pursue opportunities for state and federal appropriations, capital funding, grants, and other public resources that support University priorities. *Work collaboratively with University leadership to develop funding requests and advocacy strategies for capital projects, academic programs, student initiatives, research, workforce development, and other institutional priorities. *Coordinate governmental relations activities across the University to ensure consistent messaging, effective advocacy, and appropriate engagement with public officials. *Serve as a resource to University leaders regarding governmental processes, legislative strategy, public policy, and political and regulatory developments. *Develop and maintain an effective network of governmental and community contacts throughout the University’s service area and beyond. *Promote the University’s contributions to economic development, workforce development, research, innovation, community engagement, and the broader public good. *Collaborate with Communications and Marketing to ensure governmental relations messaging is consistent with the University’s overall communications strategy. *Ensure governmental relations activities comply with applicable federal and state laws, University policies, and ethical standards. Represent the University with professionalism, integrity, and diplomacy while maintaining appropriate political and institutional relationships. *Create partnerships within the community to provide student experiences with governmental agencies. Manage the governmental relations budget and related external consulting or advocacy resources. *Operates a university or personal vehicle safely while carrying out job responsibilities. Will authorize, pursuant to university policies and procedures, a driving record check, and will meet the driving authorization requirements through the course of employment in a position where you are authorized to drive a vehicle as part of your duties. *Carries out responsibilities in accordance with university policies and applicable laws. *Cultivates an environment of belonging that values, respects, supports, and celebrates individual similarities and differences, allowing students, faculty, and staff to thrive authentically. *Support, promote, and develop university student enrollment and retention initiatives. *Perform other duties as assigned by the President. Marginal Duties/Responsibilities: Skills and Abilities: Ability to: communicate effectively, including public speaking; influence policymakers; interact effectively with a wide variety of people and groups; plan, organize, and meet deadlines; and be creative and innovative in the development of ideas. Demonstrated successful experience working directly with people from diverse backgrounds, including cultural, educational, socioeconomic, and life experiences. The position requires the ability to build consensus, navigate complex political and institutional environments, and effectively represent the University’s interests while maintaining productive relationships with individuals and organizations holding differing perspectives. The Vice President is expected to demonstrate sound judgment, political awareness, discretion, and a strong understanding of the role of public higher education within state and federal governmental systems. Required Documents: Cover Letter Resume Unofficial Transcript 1 Optional Documents: Special Instructions to Applicants: Transcript 1 (REQUIRED): Applicants must attach a copy of unofficial transcript of Bachelor's degree. Transcript must include: Institution name, applicant name, date degree attained, degree awarded. Finalist will be required to submit an official transcript. Initial Application Review Date: October 5, 2026 Open Until Position is Filled?: Yes Posting Close Date: EEO Statement: Ferris State University, an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity employer, is committed to enhancing equity, inclusion, and diversity within its community. Ferris offers employment opportunities to qualified candidates seeking careers in a student-focused environment that values opportunity, collaboration, diversity and educational excellence. Learn more about the Ferris Mission and community at ferris.edu . The University actively seeks applications from women, minorities, individuals with disabilities, veterans, and other underrepresented groups. For more information on the University’s Policy on Non-Discrimination, visit: Ferris Non-Discrimination Statement . Click here to learn more about working at FSU and KCAD. Advertised: 04 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://jobs.ferris.edu/en-us/job/498198/assistant-director-of-residential-programs-and-services": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Residential Programs and Services",
      "description": "Assistant Director of Residential Programs and Services Apply now Job no: 498198 Work type: Staff Location: Big Rapids, MI 49307 Categories: Administrative, Staff Position Title: Assistant Director of Residential Programs and Services Location: Big Rapids (Main Campus) Department: 62102 - Housing and Residence Life Advertised Salary: $57,445 Benefits: Comprehensive benefit package (health care, vacation, etc.). Please see the following link for a list of benefits offered with this position. Admin / Admin Temp Benefit Plans FLSA: Exempt Temporary/Continuing: Continuing Part-Time/Full-Time: Full-Time Union Group: N/A Term of Position: 12 Month At Will/Just Cause: At Will Summary of Position: Provide primary responsibility for the administration of the Residence Halls, Apartments, and Suites on campus. Provide leadership and supervision for residential programs and services. Assist in the development and implementation of comprehensive policies and procedures for on-campus housing operations. The selected candidate for this position must be authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire without employer assistance or sponsorship now or in the future. The university will not assist with any F-1 EADs, OPT, H1B, etc. Position Type: Staff Required Education: Bachelor’s degree in personnel administration, counseling, or related field. Required Work Experience: 3-5 years of work experience in University housing and residence life with responsibilities which involved supervision (student or professional), corrective action, conflict resolution, and programming in a University setting. Required Licenses and Certifications: Physical Demands: Office Environment Moving Sitting Driving Repetitive movement Standing Additional Education/Experiences to be Considered: Master’s degree in student affairs leadership or related field Essential Duties/Responsibilities: • Provide leadership of the residence hall, apartment, and suite system. • Recommend for hire, train, and supervise approximately seven full-time, professional hall and community director staff. • Guide selection, training, and supervision of approximately 100 student hall staff, including Resident Advisors (RA), Community Advisors (CA), and Senior Staff Advisors (SSA), as well as approximately 50-60 Desk Services Assistants (DSA). • Develop and implement processes for effective response to crisis situations. • Facilitate and participate in on-call rotation for crisis management. • Provide supervision for desk operations in approximately 13 on-campus living communities. • Direct professional staff recruitment initiatives. • Coordinate Hall and Community Director involvement in support of other department/division operations and programs. • Advise the Residence Hall Association (RHA) student organization, which leads programming for and represents on-campus residents. • Coordinate assessment initiatives for the department. • Provide guidance for residence hall staff and students. • Serve as a referral agent to ensure student needs are met. • Develop and maintain RA Resource area and materials. • Serve as a liaison to other offices, including the Office of Student Conduct. • Develop, maintain, and supervise budget standards for individual residence hall operations. • Serve as first appeal for off-campus requests, staff discipline, and student employment issues. • Visit and inspect all on-campus living areas, monitor programming efforts, and facilitate staff meetings. • Represent the department on special committees and meetings • Assist with central office management. • Assist with operations management, facilities management, enrollment initiatives, and marketing. • Operate a university or personal vehicle safely while carrying out job responsibilities. Will authorize, pursuant to university policies and procedures, a driving record check, and will meet the driving authorization requirements through the course of employment in a position where you are authorized to drive a vehicle as part of your duties. • Carries out responsibilities in accordance with university policies and applicable laws. • Train and supervise professional and student staff on safety and emergency procedures, crisis response, mandated reporting responsibilities, incident report writing, community building, programming, and other university processes and functions to ensure the building of a highly responsive, compassionate, and responsible on-campus living environment for approximately 3,400 student residents. • Cultivates an environment of belonging that values, respects, supports, and celebrates individual similarities and differences, allowing students, faculty, and staff to thrive authentically. • Support, promote, and develop university student enrollment and retention initiatives. • Any other duties assigned within the position classification area. Marginal Duties/Responsibilities: • Serve on University committees. • Attend meetings, workshops, and conferences in absence of supervisor • Assist in the ongoing revision and development of internal and external communications Skills and Abilities: • Understand student development theory. • Demonstrate communication skills, including the ability to speak to large groups. • Work collaboratively with other University and community constituencies. • Demonstrated ability to supervise and lead a diverse group of students and staff. • Mediate conflict successfully. • Use basic computer programs. • Demonstrated successful experience working directly with people from diverse backgrounds, including cultural, educational, socioeconomic, and life experiences. Required Documents: Cover Letter Resume Unofficial Transcript 1 Optional Documents: Unofficial Transcript 2 Special Instructions to Applicants: Unofficial Transcript 1 (REQUIRED): Applicants must attach a copy of an unofficial transcript of their bachelor's degree. Transcripts must include the institution name, the applicant's name, the date the degree was attained, and the degree awarded. Finalists will be required to submit an official transcript. Unofficial Transcript 2 (OPTIONAL): Applicants who have attained a Master’s degree in student affairs leadership or related field please attach a copy of unofficial transcript. Transcript must include: Institution name, applicant name, date degree attained, degree awarded. Finalist will be required to submit an official transcript Initial Application Review Date: September 28, 2026 Open Until Position is Filled?: Yes Posting Close Date: EEO Statement: Ferris State University, an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity employer, is committed to enhancing equity, inclusion, and diversity within its community. Ferris offers employment opportunities to qualified candidates seeking careers in a student-focused environment that values opportunity, collaboration, diversity and educational excellence. Learn more about the Ferris Mission and community at ferris.edu . The University actively seeks applications from women, minorities, individuals with disabilities, veterans, and other underrepresented groups. For more information on the University’s Policy on Non-Discrimination, visit: Ferris Non-Discrimination Statement . Click here to learn more about working at FSU and KCAD. Advertised: 04 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://jobs.ferris.edu/en-us/job/498199/lgbtq-resource-student-asst-student-position": {
      "title": "LGBTQ+ Resource Student Asst (Student Position)",
      "description": "LGBTQ+ Resource Student Asst (Student Position) Apply now Job no: 498199 Work type: Student Employment - Work Study Only Location: Big Rapids, MI 49307 Categories: Position Title: LGBTQ+ Resource Center Student Assistant (Student Position) Location: Big Rapids, MI 49307 Department: LGBTQ+ Resource Center Advertised Salary: $14.00 - $14.49 Summary of Position: The LGBTQ+ resource center is seeking a student assistant. In this position, you will greet students and offer them resources. Position Requirements: Please use the \"Other Additional Document\" section to submit a letter of interest for why you want to work at the LGBTQ+ Resource Center, and any specific and relevant experiences you would like to highlight that would make you a good fit for the role. Essential Duties/Responsibilities: *Greet students and assist them with accessing the resources that the LGBTQ+ resource center offers *Be a point of contact for students visiting the center when the LGBTQ+ program coordinator is not available *Assist with the design and implementation of programs hosted by the center Number of Positions Available: 3 Documents Needed to Apply: Resume, Class Schedule, Letter of Interest Job Close Date: November 1, 2026 EEO Statement: Ferris State University, an Equal Opportunity employer, is committed to cultivating an environment of belonging that values, respects, supports, and celebrates individual similarities and differences within its community, allowing students, faculty, and staff to thrive authentically. Ferris offers employment opportunities to qualified candidates seeking careers in a student-focused environment that values opportunity, collaboration, diversity and educational excellence. Learn more about the Ferris Mission and community at ferris.edu . For more information on the University’s Policy on Non-Discrimination, visit: Ferris Non-Discrimination Statement . Advertised: 04 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 01 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://jobs.ferris.edu/en-us/job/498200/adjunct-instructor-writing-center-specialist-part-time": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor - Writing Center Specialist (Part Time)",
      "description": "Adjunct Instructor - Writing Center Specialist (Part Time) Apply now Job no: 498200 Work type: Faculty - Temporary & Continuing Location: Big Rapids, MI 49307 Categories: Faculty Position Title: Adjunct Instructor - Writing Center Specialist (Part Time) Location: Big Rapids (Main Campus) Department: 33203 - Writing Center Advertised Salary: $19.96 per hour pursuant to the FSU and FNTFO/AFT Letter of Agreement Benefits: Comprehensive benefit package (health care, vacation, etc.). Please see the following link for a list of benefits offered with this position. Adjunct Faculty FLSA: Non-Exempt Temporary/Continuing: Continuing Part-Time/Full-Time: Part-Time Union Group: Ferris Nontenure-Track Faculty Organization (FNTFO) (AFT) Term of Position: 9 Month At Will/Just Cause: Just Cause Summary of Position: Writing Center Specialists provide writing consultation (on-site and electronic/digital) to undergraduate and graduate students for 10 to 28 hours per week. The successful applicant will be familiar with APA and MLA documentation and must be comfortable working one-on-one with a diverse student population representing a full range of majors and writing skills, from remedial to advanced. The successful applicant must be comfortable working in face-to-face and electronic/digital instructional settings. This is a pooled position. One or more applicants may be hired to assist students on a semester-by-semester basis. The anticipated start date of this position will be throughout the academic year, with some initial appointments in September 2026. The selected candidate for this position must be authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire without employer assistance or sponsorship now or in the future. The university will not assist with any F-1 EADs, OPT, H1B, etc. Position Type: Faculty - Temporary & Continuing Required Education: Bachelor's degree in English, English Education, History, Social Sciences, or a related field. Required Work Experience: One year of experience involving tutoring and/or teaching at the K-12 and/or college level. Experience working with diverse student populations, including ESL. Required Licenses and Certifications: Physical Demands: Office Environment Moving Sitting Additional Education/Experiences to be Considered: Essential Duties/Responsibilities: This position requires providing writing consultation (both on-site and electronic/digital) to undergraduate and graduate students. The successful applicant will be familiar with APA and MLA documentation and must be comfortable working one-on-one with a diverse student population representing a full range of majors and writing skills. Schedule appointments that meet students' needs. Train/mentor other writing center specialists. Coordinate writing workshops with assistance of the director. Provide input to the specialists' team and director on innovative tutoring/teaching methods/modalities. Keep abreast of current writing center theory and practice. Carries out responsibilities in accordance with university policies and applicable laws. Cultivate an environment of belonging that values, respects, supports, and celebrates individual similarities and differences, allowing students, faculty, and staff to thrive authentically. Support, promote, and develop university student enrollment and retention initiatives. Any other duties assigned within the position classification area. Marginal Duties/Responsibilities: Skills and Abilities: Demonstrated successful experience working directly with people from diverse backgrounds, including cultural, educational, socioeconomic, and life experiences. Required Documents: Cover Letter Resume Unofficial Transcript 1 Optional Documents: Special Instructions to Applicants: Unofficial Transcript 1 (REQUIRED): Applicants must attach a copy of an unofficial transcript of Bachelor's degree. Transcripts must include the institution name, the applicant's name, the date the degree was attained, and the degree awarded. Finalists will be required to submit an official transcript. Initial Application Review Date: September 21, 2026 Open Until Position is Filled?: Yes Posting Close Date: EEO Statement: Ferris State University, an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity employer, is committed to enhancing equity, inclusion, and diversity within its community. Ferris offers employment opportunities to qualified candidates seeking careers in a student-focused environment that values opportunity, collaboration, diversity and educational excellence. Learn more about the Ferris Mission and community at ferris.edu . The University actively seeks applications from women, minorities, individuals with disabilities, veterans, and other underrepresented groups. For more information on the University’s Policy on Non-Discrimination, visit: Ferris Non-Discrimination Statement . Click here to learn more about working at FSU and KCAD. Advertised: 04 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://jobs.ferris.edu/en-us/job/498201/pharm-sciences-research-asst-student-position": {
      "title": "Pharm Sciences Research Asst (Student Position)",
      "description": "Pharm Sciences Research Asst (Student Position) Apply now Job no: 498201 Work type: Student Employment - General Location: Big Rapids, MI 49307 Categories: Student Employment - General Position Title: Pharmaceutical Sciences Research Assistant (Student Position) Location: Big Rapids, MI 49307 Department: College of Pharmacy Advertised Salary: $14.49 Summary of Position: Student Pharmaceutical Sciences Research Assistant — NIH-Funded Antibiotic Drug Discovery Ferris State University | College of Pharmacy Do you enjoy exploring scientific questions related to your future career? Join Dr. Eric Nybo’s laboratory and gain hands-on research experience through a National Institutes of Health (NIH)-funded antibiotic drug discovery project. This position offers an opportunity to connect classroom learning with laboratory research aimed at developing new antibiotics. Students in Pharmacy (Pharm.D.), Biotechnology, Microbiology, Chemistry, Forensic Biology, and Industrial Chemistry are especially encouraged to apply. Ideal candidates have an interest in organic chemistry, microbiology, drug discovery, and problem-solving . Responsibilities may include assisting with laboratory experiments, preparing research materials, collecting and analyzing data, maintaining accurate laboratory records, and discussing experimental results with the research team. Activities may involve microbiology, molecular biology, and natural products chemistry. Applicants should be curious, dependable, attentive to detail, and willing to learn new techniques. Relevant coursework or laboratory experience is helpful. Students will have opportunities to develop practical laboratory skills, scientific reasoning, teamwork, and communication skills relevant to their future careers. Pay: Starting at $14.49, increased for professional-level students Hours: 5 - 10 hours per week Position Requirements: Demonstrate a foundational understanding of organic chemistry, microbiology, or related subjects relevant to antibiotic drug discovery. Be able to complete detailed research assignments, follow laboratory protocols, and solve problems with appropriate guidance. Possess relevant coursework, laboratory experience, or technical skills appropriate to the assigned research activities. If University driving is required, complete a motor vehicle report authorization form. Human Resources must review the report to determine eligibility to drive on behalf of the University. Essential Duties/Responsibilities: Collect, organize, and maintain accurate research data and laboratory records. Use database and spreadsheet software to enter, manage, summarize, and analyze experimental data. Exercise sound judgment when collecting and evaluating data, identify inconsistencies, and consult the research supervisor when problems or questions arise. Number of Positions Available: 6 Documents Needed to Apply: Resume/ Class Schedule Job Close Date: Open until hired EEO Statement: Ferris State University, an Equal Opportunity employer, is committed to cultivating an environment of belonging that values, respects, supports, and celebrates individual similarities and differences within its community, allowing students, faculty, and staff to thrive authentically. Ferris offers employment opportunities to qualified candidates seeking careers in a student-focused environment that values opportunity, collaboration, diversity and educational excellence. Learn more about the Ferris Mission and community at ferris.edu . For more information on the University’s Policy on Non-Discrimination, visit: Ferris Non-Discrimination Statement . Advertised: 04 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 12 Dec 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://jobs.ferris.edu/en-us/job/498207/temporary-food-service-worker": {
      "title": "Temporary Food Service Worker",
      "description": "Temporary Food Service Worker Apply now Job no: 498207 Work type: Staff Location: Big Rapids, MI 49307 Categories: Staff, Service/Maintenance Position Title: Temporary Food Service Worker Location: Big Rapids (Main Campus) Department: 51804 - The Rock Cafe Advertised Salary: $17.53. Hourly rate pursuant to the FSU and AFSCME/AFL-CIO Agreement. Benefits: Comprehensive benefit package (health care, vacation, etc.). Please see the following link for a list of benefits offered with this position. AFSCME Benefits FLSA: Non-Exempt Temporary/Continuing: Temporary Part-Time/Full-Time: Full-Time Union Group: Custodial, Maintenance, Dining, Skilled Trade (AFSCME Local 1609) Term of Position: 12 Month At Will/Just Cause: Just Cause Summary of Position: A position classified as a Temporary Food Service Worker is available with Dining Services at The Rock Cafe. The normal working hours are 6 a.m. - 2:30 pm, Tuesday through Saturday. Days off are Sunday and Monday. The exact duration of this temporary position is undetermined at this time, but it will not exceed one year. This position will begin on September 15, or sooner, subject to approval. However, the employee may be required to work other times, including evenings and/or weekends. If required by job duties, the employee must be able to fulfill the University’s requirements concerning issuance and use of University keys. This is an AFSCME bargaining unit position. Position Type: Staff Required Education: High school diploma or GED equivalency. Required Work Experience: Six months full-time equivalent experience in commercial food service to include food preparation. Required Licenses and Certifications: Physical Demands: Bending Carrying Reaching Sitting Twisting Balancing Climbing Lifting Pulling/Pushing Repetitive movement Standing Additional Education/Experiences to be Considered: Essential Duties/Responsibilities: Provide food service support by preparing and serving food, waiting on customers, and maintaining the assigned area. As directed, train others in the performance of the characteristic duties. Assist other food service employees as needed. Assure that food is palatable and appetizing. Cook and handle food in accordance with safe food-handling and sanitary requirements, following departmental sanitation policies. Interact with customers and staff in a polite, friendly, and courteous manner. Maintain equipment, pots, pans, utensils, and the work area in a clean, orderly, and sanitary condition in accordance with departmental sanitation policies. Minimize food waste. Obtain foods from storage areas. Operate various kitchen equipment and machines, utilize utensils. Perform the work of other Dining Service classifications when assigned. Prepare food for service and ensure continued availability. Prepare foods using recipes generated from the computerized menu management system. Respond to special dietary requests. Set up and maintain food preparation, serving, and display areas in an attractive and sanitary manner. Maintain safety and sanitation standards in all duties and responsibilities. Report to immediate supervisor and/or designee. Train and direct the work of student employees in performing the characteristic duties. Carries out responsibilities in accordance with university policies and applicable laws. Cultivates an environment of belonging that values, respects, supports, and celebrates individual similarities and differences, allowing students, faculty, and staff to thrive authentically. Support, promote, and develop university student enrollment and retention initiatives. Any other duties assigned within the position classification area. Marginal Duties/Responsibilities: Remove trash, break down cardboard boxes, and assist in the University recycling efforts. Skills and Abilities: Ability to communicate effectively. Ability to interact with the public in a friendly, polite, and courteous manner. Be familiar with seasoning requirements and kitchen equipment. Cook a variety of foods in large and small quantities. Follow safety and sanitation standards and practices. Keep accurate records. Read, understand, and follow recipes, directions, safety labels, equipment operations manuals, etc. Work harmoniously with others in variable temperatures, close confines, noise, and stressful situations and workloads. Demonstrated successful experience working directly with people from diverse backgrounds, including cultural, educational, socioeconomic, and life experiences. Required Documents: Cover Letter Resume Optional Documents: Special Instructions to Applicants: All applicants must ensure their application information reflects how they meet the minimum qualifications posted in the job posting to be considered for the position. Initial Application Review Date: September 21, 2026 Open Until Position is Filled?: Yes Posting Close Date: EEO Statement: Ferris State University, an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity employer, is committed to enhancing equity, inclusion, and diversity within its community. Ferris offers employment opportunities to qualified candidates seeking careers in a student-focused environment that values opportunity, collaboration, diversity and educational excellence. Learn more about the Ferris Mission and community at ferris.edu . The University actively seeks applications from women, minorities, individuals with disabilities, veterans, and other underrepresented groups. For more information on the University’s Policy on Non-Discrimination, visit: Ferris Non-Discrimination Statement . Click here to learn more about working at FSU and KCAD. Advertised: 04 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
