    "https://jobs.ferris.edu/en-us/job/498086/pharmacy-tutor-2-student-position": {
      "title": "Pharmacy Tutor 2 (Student Position)",
      "description": "Pharmacy Tutor 2 (Student Position) Apply now Job no: 498086 Work type: Student Employment - General Location: Big Rapids, MI 49307 Categories: Student Employment - General Position Title: Pharmacy Tutor II (Student Position) Location: Big Rapids, MI Department: Pharmacy Advertised Salary: $14.00- $14.49 Summary of Position: Level II tutors must have experience assisting students with course work in various subject areas. The Pharmacy Tutor provides supplemental academic support through tutoring activities designed to reinforce course concepts and aid student mastery of course content. This position supports, but does not replace, instructional faculty. This position is not eligible for remote work. All compensated work must be performed from an approved university location. The position may include virtual delivery of tutoring to students; however, virtual delivery must occur while the student employee is physically working from an approved university location. Student employees in this role are not instructors and may not perform duties reserved for instructional faculty, including creating or revising syllabi, designing or assigning graded coursework, assessing learning for grades, grading student work, managing Canvas or other LMS course functions, delivering required instruction on behalf of faculty, substituting for faculty, making course policy decisions, or completing compensated work already assigned to instructional faculty. The role is limited to supplemental academic support, facilitation of learning activities, and student-success support. Position Requirements: All tutors must have a GPA of 2.75 or better and have completed the course they are assisting with and received a B or better. Essential Duties/Responsibilities: - Excellent communications skills - Organization. - Supervisor may assign various duties if additional tasks must be completed. *Assist students with course work in various subject areas Number of Positions Available: 5 Documents Needed to Apply: Resume/Class Schedule Job Close Date: November 20, 2026 EEO Statement: Ferris State University, an Equal Opportunity employer, is committed to cultivating an environment of belonging that values, respects, supports, and celebrates individual similarities and differences within its community, allowing students, faculty, and staff to thrive authentically. Ferris offers employment opportunities to qualified candidates seeking careers in a student-focused environment that values opportunity, collaboration, diversity and educational excellence. Learn more about the Ferris Mission and community at ferris.edu . For more information on the University’s Policy on Non-Discrimination, visit: Ferris Non-Discrimination Statement . Advertised: 10 Aug 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 20 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://jobs.ferris.edu/en-us/job/498092/facilitiestransport-clerical-student-position": {
      "title": "Facilities-Transport Clerical (Student Position)",
      "description": "Facilities-Transport Clerical (Student Position) Apply now Job no: 498092 Work type: Student Employment - General Location: Big Rapids, MI 49307 Categories: Student Employment - General Position Title: Facilities - Transportation Office Clerical (Student Position) Location: Big Rapids, MI Department: Campus Services Advertised Salary: $13.73-$13.99 Summary of Position: The Transportation Office is looking for students to fill their clerical position. The position will consist of assisting the department with office work. Position Requirements: Very detailed and accurate Should have strong communication and customer service skills Have knowledge of Microsoft programs such as Word and Excel Must meet FSU student driving requirements - As an individual who is responsible for driving on behalf of the university, a driving record check will be run pursuant to university policies. The individual will be required to sign an authorization to run a criminal history background and driving record check. Essential Duties/Responsibilities: - Position will assist with multiple support functions for our Transportation Office, including - work order creation, - answering phones - filing - maintaining spreadsheets - May have to run documents and other paperwork to different buildings as needed. - Customer Service - Other duties as assigned Number of Positions Available: 2 Documents Needed to Apply: Resume/ Class schedule Job Close Date: December 12, 2026 EEO Statement: Ferris State University, an Equal Opportunity employer, is committed to cultivating an environment of belonging that values, respects, supports, and celebrates individual similarities and differences within its community, allowing students, faculty, and staff to thrive authentically. Ferris offers employment opportunities to qualified candidates seeking careers in a student-focused environment that values opportunity, collaboration, diversity and educational excellence. Learn more about the Ferris Mission and community at ferris.edu . For more information on the University’s Policy on Non-Discrimination, visit: Ferris Non-Discrimination Statement . Advertised: 10 Aug 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 12 Dec 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://jobs.ferris.edu/en-us/job/498062/administrator-of-student-success-initiatives-4s-programs": {
      "title": "Administrator of Student Success Initiatives, 4S Programs",
      "description": "Administrator of Student Success Initiatives, 4S Programs Apply now Job no: 498062 Work type: Staff Location: Big Rapids, MI 49307 Categories: Administrative, Staff Position Title: Administrator of Student Success Initiatives, 4S Programs Location: Big Rapids (Main Campus) Department: 43000 - University College Advertised Salary: $41,272-$44,129. Salary commensurate with education, experiences and other requirements. Benefits: Comprehensive benefit package (health care, vacation, etc.). Please see the following link for a list of benefits offered with this position. Admin / Admin Temp Benefit Plans FLSA: Exempt Temporary/Continuing: Temporary Part-Time/Full-Time: Full-Time Union Group: N/A Term of Position: 12 Month At Will/Just Cause: At Will Summary of Position: The Administrator of Student Success Initiatives serves as the primary lead for the Determined Dawgs Scholar Academy (DDSA), a comprehensive summer bridge and transition program designed to promote the successful transition, persistence, and academic achievement of students with elevated support needs. As a member of the Office of Student Success Initiatives, the administrator provides leadership for the planning, implementation, assessment, and continuous improvement of DDSA and related student success initiatives. The position develops and facilitates educational programming, coordinates holistic student support services, collaborates with campus partners, and utilizes data to evaluate program effectiveness and inform strategic decision making. The administrator also supports grant implementation, compliance, assessment, continuous improvement, reporting, and grant solicitation and continuation while advancing institutional priorities related to student success, retention, belonging, and degree completion. This is a temporary, grant-funded position through September 30, 2029. Continued employment is contingent upon successful performance and the availability of grant funding. The anticipated start date of this position is September 2026. However, this is an estimated date and can be revised if needed. The selected candidate for this position must be authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire without employer assistance or sponsorship now or in the future. The university will not assist with any F-1 EADs, OPT, H1B, etc. Position Type: Staff Required Education: Bachelor's degree in Higher Education, Student Affairs, Education, Counseling, Social Work, Psychology, or a closely related field. Required Work Experience: • Experience coordinating student success, transition, retention, advising, academic support, educational programming, or related initiatives within higher education. • Experience developing collaborative partnerships and working with students from a variety of educational, socioeconomic, and cultural backgrounds. Required Licenses and Certifications: Physical Demands: Office Environment Carrying Reaching Sitting Twisting Repetitive movement Standing Additional Education/Experiences to be Considered: • Master's degree in Higher Education, Student Affairs, Counseling, Education, Leadership, or a related field preferred. • Experience with grant administration, program assessment, and project management. • Experience teaching college success, first-year seminar, student development, orientation, or transition courses. • Experience utilizing student success technologies, early alert systems, and data to inform student support strategies. Essential Duties/Responsibilities: Student Success Program Leadership: • Provide leadership for the planning, implementation, and continuous improvement of the Determined Dawgs Scholar Academy (DDSA), including the four-week summer bridge experience and the associated three-semester student success seminar sequence. • Develop, facilitate, and assess educational experiences that promote academic success, college readiness, belonging, resource utilization, leadership development, and successful transition to Ferris State University. • Coordinate student engagement activities, workshops, campus experiences, and educational programming that reinforce program learning outcomes and contribute to student persistence and degree completion. • Recruit, train, supervise, and evaluate student leaders, peer mentors, or Pack Leaders who support DDSA programming and student success initiatives. • Monitor student participation, academic progress, and engagement while connecting students with appropriate campus resources through proactive outreach and coordinated intervention strategies. Strategic Partnerships and Student Support: • Develop and maintain collaborative relationships with faculty, academic advisors, student affairs professionals, enrollment services, and campus partners to ensure coordinated support for participating students. • Work collaboratively with offices including Academic Advising, Financial Aid, Disability and Accessibility Resources, Career and Professional Success, Housing and Residence Life, Counseling, and other campus partners to promote holistic student success. • Represent Student Success Initiatives and DDSA through campus committees, outreach activities, recruitment events, presentations, and professional conferences. Assessment, Data, and Continuous Improvement: • Develop and implement assessment plans that evaluate student learning, program participation, and program effectiveness. • Collect, analyze, interpret, and communicate student success data to support evidence-informed decision making and continuous improvement. • Prepare reports, presentations, and recommendations for university leadership and funding agencies regarding program outcomes and effectiveness. • Collaborate with Institutional Research and other campus partners to ensure accurate collection, analysis, and reporting of student success data while maintaining compliance with institutional privacy requirements. Grant Administration and Fiscal Stewardship: • Support implementation of all activities associated with the KCP Select Student Support Services (4S) Grant. • Assist with grant reporting, documentation, compliance monitoring, budget management, and evaluation activities to ensure adherence to grant requirements and university policies. • Collaborate with university leadership in identifying opportunities for external funding and sustainability of student success initiatives. Institutional Student Success Initiatives: • Support university-wide retention, persistence, enrollment, and student success initiatives that advance the mission of the Office of Student Success Initiatives. • Participate in strategic planning, program development, and continuous improvement efforts that strengthen institutional support for students with elevated academic, financial, social, or transitional support needs. • Utilize institutional technologies, including Navigate360 and other student success platforms, to document services, monitor student engagement, coordinate interventions, and evaluate program outcomes. General Responsibilities: • Carries out responsibilities in accordance with university policies and applicable laws. • Cultivates an environment of belonging that values, respects, supports, and celebrates individual similarities and differences, allowing students, faculty, and staff to thrive authentically. • Support, promote, and develop university student enrollment and retention initiatives. • Any other duties assigned within the position classification area. Marginal Duties/Responsibilities: Skills and Abilities: • Demonstrated knowledge of student development theory, student success strategies, transition programming, retention initiatives, and holistic student support practices. • Strong organizational, project management, and problem-solving skills with the ability to manage multiple priorities simultaneously. • Excellent written, verbal, interpersonal, and presentation skills. • Ability to develop collaborative partnerships across academic and student affairs. • Experience interpreting and utilizing quantitative and qualitative data to evaluate program effectiveness and guide decision making. • Demonstrated ability to independently manage projects while contributing effectively as a collaborative team member. • Knowledge of assessment methodologies, continuous improvement practices, and evidence-informed program development. • Demonstrated successful experience working directly with people from diverse backgrounds, including cultural, educational, socioeconomic, and life experiences. Required Documents: Cover Letter Resume Unofficial Transcript 1 Optional Documents: Unofficial Transcript 2 Special Instructions to Applicants: Unofficial Transcript 1 (REQUIRED): Applicants must attach a copy of an unofficial transcript of their Bachlelor's degree. Transcripts must include the institution name, the applicant's name, the date the degree was attained, and the degree awarded. Finalists will be required to submit an official transcript. Unofficial Transcript 2 (OPTIONAL): Applicants who have completed college coursework or attained a Master's degree must attach a copy of their unofficial transcript. Transcripts must include the institution name, the applicant's name, the date the degree was attained, and the degree awarded. Finalists will be required to submit an official transcript. Initial Application Review Date: August 24, 2026 Open Until Position is Filled?: Yes Posting Close Date: EEO Statement: Ferris State University, an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity employer, is committed to enhancing equity, inclusion, and diversity within its community. Ferris offers employment opportunities to qualified candidates seeking careers in a student-focused environment that values opportunity, collaboration, diversity and educational excellence. Learn more about the Ferris Mission and community at ferris.edu . The University actively seeks applications from women, minorities, individuals with disabilities, veterans, and other underrepresented groups. For more information on the University’s Policy on Non-Discrimination, visit: Ferris Non-Discrimination Statement . Click here to learn more about working at FSU and KCAD. Advertised: 07 Aug 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://jobs.ferris.edu/en-us/job/498064/preparator": {
      "title": "Preparator",
      "description": "Preparator Apply now Job no: 498064 Work type: Staff Location: Grand Rapids, MI 49503 Categories: Administrative, Staff Position Title: Preparator Location: Grand Rapids (Kendall) Department: 92600 - KCAD Admin and Finance Advertised Salary: $17.51 per hour. Benefits: Comprehensive benefit package (health care, vacation, etc.). Please see the following link for a list of benefits offered with this position. Admin / Admin Temp Benefit Plans FLSA: Non-Exempt Temporary/Continuing: Temporary Part-Time/Full-Time: Full-Time Union Group: N/A Term of Position: 12 Month At Will/Just Cause: Just Cause Summary of Position: The Preparator will work closely with the Exhibitions Director to produce high-quality exhibitions. The Preparator assists in planning, preparation, security, installation, and de-installation of gallery exhibitions and other display materials. Key responsibilities include training and supervising temporary work crews and student gallery workers on gallery installations, preparing and maintaining exhibitions, and managing storage and preparatory spaces. The anticipated start date of this position is August 2026; however, this is an estimated date and can be revised if needed. The selected candidate for this position must be authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire without employer assistance or sponsorship now or in the future. The university will not assist with any F-1 EADs, OPT, H1B, etc. Position Type: Staff Required Education: BA or BFA in Art, Art History, Museum Studies, BA in Design, or related field. Required Work Experience: At least 2 years of progressively responsible experience in art handling, gallery/museum work, or fabrication/installation, with an emphasis on preparation/installation strongly preferred. Required Licenses and Certifications: Scissor Lift Certification, or ability to obtain certification within 3 months of hire. Physical Demands: Bending Carrying Moving Reaching Sitting Twisting Balancing Climbing Driving Heights Lifting Pulling/Pushing Repetitive movement Standing Additional Education/Experiences to be Considered: Essential Duties/Responsibilities: Coordinate, oversee, and execute logistics of all exhibition installations and deinstallations, working alongside the Exhibitions Director. Track supplies and resource needs and determine adjustments in stock quantities as required. Monitor the condition and appearance of exhibitions, identify problem areas, recommend solutions, and oversee necessary repairs and maintenance, or coordinate work with other departments as required. Oversee the maintenance and repair of gallery spaces, tools, equipment, and supplies. Ensure adherence to safe workplace practices, including using the lift only after university certification and following procedures for handling art and for all preparation work and workshop spaces. Coordinate and execute the packing, labeling, shipping, and local transport of art and exhibitions. Condition all incoming and outgoing artworks to ensure that works are documented and that no damage occurs during their loan period. Fabricate, plan, and purchase materials for exhibition assets/display purposes. Monitor environmental needs in gallery space. Knowledge of various Technology and AV setups. Operate a university or personal vehicle safely while carrying out job responsibilities. Will authorize, pursuant to university policies and procedures, a driving record check, and will meet the driving authorization requirements through the course of employment in a position where you are authorized to drive a vehicle as part of our duties. Carries out responsibilities in accordance with university policies and applicable laws. Cultivates an environment of belonging that values, respects, supports, and celebrates individual similarities and differences, allowing students, faculty, and staff to thrive authentically. Supports, promotes, and develops university student enrollment and retention initiatives. Any other duties assigned within the position classification area. Marginal Duties/Responsibilities: Assist in the development of exhibition and special project budgets. Coordinate and manufacture exhibition-related signage and labels. Train, monitor, manage, schedule, and work alongside on-call preparators and student preparators. Skills and Abilities: Strong skills in art preparation, carpentry, building, and painting. Excellent communication and problem-solving skills. Meticulous attention to detail. Project management skills. Ability to work collaboratively with a team. Demonstrated successful experience working directly with people from diverse backgrounds, including cultural, educational, socioeconomic, and life experiences. Required Documents: Cover Letter Resume Unofficial Transcript 1 Optional Documents: Visual portfolio of exhibition or fabrication projects Any certifications or licenses Special Instructions to Applicants: Unofficial Transcript 1 (REQUIRED): Applicants must attach a copy of an unofficial transcript of their degree. Transcripts must include the institution name, the applicant's name, the date the degree was attained, and the degree awarded. Finalists will be required to submit an official transcript. Initial Application Review Date: August 24, 2026 Open Until Position is Filled?: Yes Posting Close Date: EEO Statement: Ferris State University, an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity employer, is committed to enhancing equity, inclusion, and diversity within its community. Ferris offers employment opportunities to qualified candidates seeking careers in a student-focused environment that values opportunity, collaboration, diversity and educational excellence. Learn more about the Ferris Mission and community at ferris.edu . The University actively seeks applications from women, minorities, individuals with disabilities, veterans, and other underrepresented groups. For more information on the University’s Policy on Non-Discrimination, visit: Ferris Non-Discrimination Statement . Click here to learn more about working at FSU and KCAD. Advertised: 07 Aug 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
